  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Current radar loop is showing showers\nand thunderstorms developing across central Nevada this afternoon.\nSome of these cells, especially those moving across White Pine\ncounty, are more wet than dry per radar estimation and the latest\nweb cams looking over US Highways 6, 50, and 93. Additional cells\nare developing over portions of Lincoln and southern Nye counties\nand those are also moving north. These are all in response to \nsubtropical moisture streaming northward throughout the day. A \nstrong upper ridge is maintaining its position across the four \ncorners region with an upper trough off the coast of the Pacific \nNorthwest. The resulting southerly flow has allowed the \nprecipitable water values to come up to at or above 0.75 inches in\ncentral Nevada. This moist air mass will make its way north \nduring the evening and overnight hours. Look for the lows to be in\nthe 50s and 60s.\n\nLittle movement is expected concerning the main weather features \nfor tomorrow. The upper ridge will shift slightly to the east \nwhile the upper trough will be moving to the northeast and \nimpacting northwest Washington State. Moisture will remain rather\nhigher than average with precipitable water values at or above \n0.75 inches. Surface-based CAPE values will be around 250 to \n700J/kg with lifted indices below 0C. This will translate into an \nexpansion of showers and thunderstorms across northern and central\nNevada. Some of these storms will be capable of brief moderate to\nheavy rain with any of these cells. There is a hydrologic concern\ndue to the fairly robust moisture values and will need to\ncarefully watch recent burn scars for possible debris flows. Wind\ngusts to 50 mph will also be possible along with dangerous cloud \nto ground lightning strikes. High temperatures will be slightly \ncooler with readings in the low to mid 90s. Look for storms to \ncontinue during the overnight with lows in the 50s.\n\nThe upper level ridge will wobble slightly east across New Mexico\nand western Texas on Sunday and Monday. Southwest flow aloft will\ntry to bring in drier air but it will be slow to push the moisture\nout. Expecting a continuation of showers and thunderstorms Sunday\nafternoon with more limited coverage to just northern Elko county\non Monday. Highs will be cooling with readings in the 80s and 90s\non both days with lows in the 50s.\n\nThe upper ridge is forecast to gradually retrograde west from\nTuesday through the end of the forecast period. The flow is\nexpected to come from a more westerly direction as the ridge of\nforecast to be suppressed south by an upper trough to the north.\nThis flow will help to keep a lid on any active weather during\nthis period. Highs will be in the 80s and 90s through this time\nwith lows in the 50s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: There is high confidence for \nthe return of southerly flow and monsoonal moisture persisting\nthrough the weekend, bringing a daily 20% to 50% chance for \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms for Nevada through\nWednesday.\n\nThere is low confidence of upper pattern evolution after \nWednesday as models show either continued monsoon flow or a return\nof the upper level ridge and warmer temperatures.",
